GUI modernization, Phase 4 (third slice): desktop/src/i18n/generated/rules.json
(the last item ADR 0004 named as "not started" besides Rust/Go error codes
and the untagged-Latin scanner) plus the actual frontend wiring the first
Phase 4 slice built the Go/API side for but never used -
IncidentCard.tsx's title/advice/per-clause-why and Rules.tsx's title now
render `i18n.ar` instead of the raw English rule fields.

## 1. Why this needed a real Go program, not a second YAML parser

`internal/correlate/gen/main.go` (new, `go run ./internal/correlate/gen`)
loads `rules/*.yaml` through `correlate.LoadRules` - the exact function
`netrewindd` and `netrewind rules` themselves call, strict decoder and
`Validate()` included - rather than a second, looser YAML read. A rule
that would fail to load for the recorder cannot end up looking fine in
the generated GUI catalogue; the generator and the engine agree by
construction, not by two implementations staying in sync by hand.
Mirrors internal/event/gen's own reasoning for kinds.json exactly.

Output shape matches `internal/api/v1/server.go`'s `ruleSummary` field
for field (confirmed by reading it directly before writing the
generator), so a live-fetched rule and a generated one are the same
TypeScript type (`RuleSummary`, `desktop/src/data/types.ts`, which
gained the `i18n` field this slice added - it did not exist on the
frontend type at all before this, confirmed by grep before assuming).

    $ go run ./internal/correlate/gen
    wrote 19 rules to .../desktop/src/i18n/generated/rules.json

`internal/correlate/rules_catalogue_test.go`'s
`TestTheGeneratedRulesCatalogueIsUpToDate` re-loads the real rules
directory through the same `LoadRules` call and fails on any drift -
proven by corrupting one field in the checked-in JSON and watching it
fail with the exact diff, then restoring and watching it pass again.

## 2. The frontend lookup module - and the one design decision worth recording

`desktop/src/i18n/rulesCatalogue.ts`: `findRule(ruleId, liveRules)` prefers
a live `/v1/rules` entry over the static snapshot (a live recorder can be
pointed at a customised rules directory the frontend was never built
against); `titleFor`/`adviceFor`/`whyFor` each take the incident's own
already-known English text as an explicit `fallback` parameter and
**never** substitute the rule's own current field when no translation
exists - only ever that fallback. This matters for an old bundle: an
incident's `title`/`advice`/`why` were copied into it verbatim at the
moment it was built (`engine.go`'s `build()`) and are the authoritative
record of what actually fired; the rule file today could have since been
reworded, and silently swapping in "what the rule currently says" in
place of "what this incident actually said" would misrepresent history
for the sake of an English string that did not even need translating.
`whyFor` specifically keys by `rule_id + clause` (the clause's own `as`,
ADR 0004's design from the first Phase 4 slice) and falls back to the
existing `link.why` whenever `clause` is absent or unrecognised - which
current demo data always is (§4 below).

`desktop/src/i18n/rulesCatalogue.test.ts`: 11 tests against a rule that
really shipped (`address-changed-hands`), not a synthetic fixture -
proves the static snapshot returns real translated content, proves live
beats static when both know a rule, proves every one of the three
functions falls through to the caller's fallback (not the rule's own
field) when undefined/untranslated/unmatched. The "never returns the
rule's current field" guard was deliberately broken
(`titleFor` changed to `?? rule?.title ?? fallback`) and watched fail
with the exact wrong value before being reverted - not merely asserted
and trusted.

## 3. Frontend wiring

- `desktop/src/types.ts`: `IncidentLink` gained `clause?: string`
  (`internal/incident/incident.go`'s `Link.Clause`, JSON `clause,omitempty` -
  added to the Go struct in the first Phase 4 slice, never added to the
  TS type until now, confirmed by grep before assuming it existed).
- `IncidentCard.tsx`: takes a new `rules: RuleSummary[]` prop; incident
  title, advice (when present) and every chain link's `why` now go
  through `findRule`/`titleFor`/`adviceFor`/`whyFor` instead of rendering
  the raw incident field. The two real call sites
  (`pages/Incidents.tsx`, `onboarding/steps/SampleInvestigationStep.tsx`)
  and their own callers (`App.tsx`, `onboarding/Wizard.tsx`) updated to
  pass `record.rules` through - four one-line changes, not a redesign.
- `Rules.tsx`: the live-catalogue branch's title now goes through
  `titleFor`. The demo/bundle "only fired rules are known" branch
  previously showed a bare rule ID in every case, in both languages -
  now looks the id up in the static snapshot and shows its title
  (translated when Arabic) beside the id when known, unchanged (bare id
  only) when not. This is a real capability demo mode did not have
  before, not only a translation of one it already had.

